From 2e0a95938cf1f81b55ddf1ac01f4366b48581471 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hyperagent Developer Agent <oliiwjqc@polosmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 01:02:56 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] feat(farm2): a live lane writes its decide-time skips as the
 rows legacy writes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes FARM2-VERIFICATION §7.8. A live lane re-decides every campaign every
cycle, but a campaign it decided NOT to farm left no AutoFarmTask row at all:
the decision lived only in FarmJob, invisible to the Auto-farm tab, to replay
and to anyone reading the shared collection. The World of Tanks incident set
the rule for closing that — any row the lane writes must be one legacy already
produces, because legacy's fleet sweeps read status and act on it.

THE MAPPING IS THE IDENTITY ON `decision`. Since the six gates shipped the
lane's skip vocabulary is legacy's, so every lane skip has a legacy equivalent
and there is no lane skip without one. What differs per decision is the FIELD
SET legacy's record() writes, which execute.legacySkipFields copies exactly:

  skip_low_demand, skip_probe_budget   demandScore = EFFECTIVE demand
                                        (alloc.demand — not the raw score),
                                        hadResearch, internalSales
  skip_host_offline, skip_ends_soon    demandScore raw, hadResearch,
                                        internalSales
  skip_already_covered                 + coverage
  skip_no_accounts                     + coverage, plannedAccounts 0
  skip_no_capacity                     + coverage, plannedAccounts = target
  skip_reuse_only                      the claim-time END state: plannedAccounts
                                        0, targetAccounts (the farm record's
                                        wanted), coverage, executedAt, dryRun
                                        false

Common to all: status "skipped" (the one status no sweep acts on), reason,
dryRun from af, rescanRequested false, decidedAt, decisionInputs. Nothing
else — no targetAccounts where legacy writes none, no executedAt except where
legacy stamps it.

THE ONE EDGE: skip_reuse_only. Legacy never decides it; it records a farm row,
the claim finds none of the game's own recycled accounts, and executeTask
rewrites the row. The lane decides it up front (the six-gates pre-check) and
writes that end state directly. Under af.dryRun legacy never reaches the
claim — it records a planned farm — so no legacy row corresponds and the lane
writes nothing there. That is the single "no equivalent" case; absence is its
answer.

THE GUARD. A skip row is written only when the (game, campaignId) row is
absent or already skipped. Never over planned, active, completed, stopped or
failed — those own bots, accounts and a history, and a $set of status
"skipped" over one would orphan them. A live lane re-decides an ACTIVE
campaign as skip_already_covered routinely, once its own accounts cover the
demand, so this case is ordinary, not hypothetical. Deterministic pre-check;
conditional upsert on status "skipped"; the unique (game, campaignId) index
turns a concurrent non-skipped insert into a duplicate-key error that is
treated as suppressed, never as a write. Suppressions are counted on the lane
summary (skipsRecorded / skipsSuppressed).

SHADOW LANES WRITE NOTHING. Legacy is still farming a shadow lane's game and
writing its rows; an upsert would overwrite legacy's decision. recordSkip
asserts on it as executeDecision does, and lane.js never calls it off the live
path.

Stated difference from legacy: legacy re-decides only RETRYABLE skips; the
lane decides everything every cycle (its design, not this commit's), so a
terminal skip row is rewritten each cycle here. Same shape, no reader — churn,
not risk; the knob if it matters is "write only on change".

Twelve tests: the mapping over the whole vocabulary; effective-vs-raw demand;
per-decision fields against legacy's record() calls; the guard against every
non-skipped status; rewrite of a skipped row on re-decision; the reuse-only
edge in both modes; the shadow assertion; and five end-to-end runs through the
real runLane — a sellability skip landing as a legacy row, skip_host_offline
with no host, re-decision rewriting in place with a fresh snapshot, a shadow
lane writing nothing, and a live lane NOT clobbering an ACTIVE task it
re-decides as a skip.

Suite 719/720 vs 707/708; the single failure is unchanged. autoFarmer.js and
autoLister.js untouched.
